
Q1. Explain the four pillars of OOPS —
 Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction. Give a real-world example for each.

 Ans -> Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming approach based on classes and objects.
  Its four main pillars are Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.

  (i) Encapsulation -> Encapsulation is process of wrapping data into single unit and
                       restricting direct access using private keyword.

  (ii) Inheritance -> Inheritance allows one class to acquire the properties and methods of another class.
                      It promotes code reusability.

  (iii) Polymorphism -> Polymorphism means one name, many forms.
                        The same method name or interface can behave differently depending on the context.

  (vi) Abstraction -> Abstraction means hiding implementation details and showing only essential functionality.

